@database "simple_prompt_inject"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: simple_prompt_inject.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Simple Prompt Inject"
@{h1}Simple Prompt Inject

Puts one fixed piece of text in front of the model before every LLM call --
a standing instruction, a reminder, a house rule -- without editing any
agent's system prompt. The text comes from the plugin's settings or from a
Markdown file, may use the agent's template variables, and goes in as one
message at a place you choose: right behind the system prompt, just before
the last user message, or at the end.

The shipped configuration has it on for every agent, sub-agents and chat
agents included, with one line right behind the system prompt:

@{code text}
If you start to repeat yourself with tool calls or thinking, try a different approach.
@{body}

The plugin has no tools and no panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  Where the text goes  " link position}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The hook  " link hook}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}, as shipped:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    simple_prompt_inject:
      type: simple_prompt_inject
      enabled: true
      config:
        prompt_text: |
          If you start to repeat yourself with tool calls or thinking, try a different approach.
        injection_position: "after_system" # or "before_last_user" or "end"
        # make it at system role, else the llm is confused about injections
        role: "system"
@{body}

No allowlist line -- there are no tools. No extra packages. The hook is on
for every agent as soon as the entry is enabled; see
@{" The hook " link hook} for switching it off for one agent.

@{h2}Settings

They go under @{tt}config:@{utt}, are read once when the plugin starts and
hold for every agent; a change takes effect after a restart.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
prompt_text | empty | the text; empty means nothing is injected
prompt_file | empty | a Markdown file whose content is the text; relative to the installation's @{tt}config/@{utt} directory, or absolute. When set, it replaces prompt_text -- an empty file injects nothing
injection_position | before_last_user | @{tt}after_system@{utt}, @{tt}task_start@{utt}, @{tt}before_last_user@{utt} or @{tt}end@{utt}; see @{" Where the text goes " link position}
role | developer | @{tt}developer@{utt}, @{tt}user@{utt} or @{tt}system@{utt}
@{body}

An unknown position or role is logged as an error and the default is used.
@{tt}role: system@{utt} always stands behind the system prompt: with another
position the plugin logs an error and places it there anyway.
@{tt}task_start@{utt} writes into the user's task, so its role is
@{tt}user@{utt}; another role is logged as an error and ignored.

@{h2}The file

@{tt}prompt_file@{utt} is read once, when the plugin starts. HTML comments
(@{tt}<!-- ... -->@{utt}) are removed from it -- notes for whoever edits the
file never reach the model; @{tt}prompt_text@{utt} is sent as written. A
file that does not exist stops this plugin from starting ("Failed to register
plugin simple_prompt_inject" in the log, with the path); everything else
starts.

@{h2}Template variables

The text is a Jinja template. The variables are the agent's
@{tt}template_vars@{utt} from its YAML, overridden key by key by the
session's own variables (@{tt}/vars KEY=VALUE@{utt} in the chat):

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  template_vars:
    lang: "German"
@{body}

@{code yaml}
prompt_text: "Answer in {{ lang }}."
@{body}

@{bullet}Without any variable at all the text is sent as it is, braces
included.
@{bullet}A variable that does not exist renders as nothing.
@{bullet}A text that is not valid Jinja is sent as written, with a warning in
the log.
@{bullet}A text that renders empty sends nothing, and takes out the copy an
earlier call put in.

Only these variables exist here -- not the date, the tool list or the other
values the system prompt template gets. Nothing in the text changes with time
unless one of your variables does.

@{h2}Several texts

One entry holds one text. For a second one, add a second entry with its own
name:

@{code yaml}
    style_reminder:
      type: simple_prompt_inject
      enabled: true
      config:
        prompt_text: "Keep answers short."
        injection_position: "before_last_user"
@{body}

Its hook is @{tt}style_reminder.inject_prompt@{utt}, and its message is
marked with that name, so the two never replace each other. This is also the
way to give some agents a different text: the agent's override can switch a
hook on or off, it cannot change the text.
@endnode

@node position "Where the text goes"
@{h1}Where the text goes

@{table}
Position | Where | What a new turn does
after_system | right behind the system prompt (behind every system message at the head) | nothing: same place, same text
task_start | in front of the task, inside the first user message a caller wrote | nothing: same place, same text
before_last_user | just in front of the last message the model is asked to answer | the text moves in front of the new message
end | appended at the end of the list | depends on the role, see below
@{body}

@{h2}after_system

Part of the instructions. It stays where it is for the whole session and is
written again only when the rendered text changes -- and then the whole
history behind it has to be sent to the provider uncached once. Keep a text
built from variables stable within a session. This is the position to use
for a standing rule.

@{h2}task_start

The text stands at the start of the task itself, followed by a
@{tt}---@{utt} line -- the first user message that no hook put there. The
message stays the user's: it keeps no @{tt}injected_by@{utt} mark, so turn
counters and @{tt}tool_preload@{utt} still find the task. What the hook
wrote is recorded on the message (@{tt}prefixed_by@{utt}, saved with the
session, never sent): an unchanged text is left alone, a changed one
replaces the old, an empty one is taken out. Several entries can write in
front of the same task. A copy the same entry left at another position
(the setting changed over a restart) is taken out; a task sent back with
the text already in front (a retry from the web chat) is taken over, not
written twice; an archive placeholder of @{tt}context_engineer@{utt} is
never the task. @{tt}tool_preload@{utt} matches its rules on the task
without the text. On writer agents a list of overused motifs was
followed a little more here than as a message of its own; the difference
was not significant.

@{h2}before_last_user

For a reminder the model should read just before it answers. The anchor is
the last user message of any kind -- one a person typed, a delivered direct
message, a scripted follow-up -- or the wake of a woken run; without one the
text is appended. Within a turn it stays put while tool results pile up
behind it. With the next turn it moves: the copy in front of the old message
goes, a new one goes in front of the new message. Everything from the old
copy on -- the whole previous turn, with its tool calls -- is then no longer
cached.

@{h2}end

Appended once, then left alone. A changed text is appended again behind the
old copy, which stays. What happens at the next turn depends on the role:

@{bullet}@{tt}user@{utt}: the message is saved with the session and stays
where it was appended, for good. The most cache-friendly choice after
@{tt}after_system@{utt} -- but the text stands in the history at the place
it first appeared, not at the end.
@{bullet}@{tt}developer@{utt}: a developer note is not saved with the
session. The next turn appends it anew, behind the new user message, and the
previous turn is no longer cached from the old copy on.
@endnode

@node hook "The hook"
@{h1}The hook

@{tt}inject_prompt@{utt}, a @{tt}pre_llm_call@{utt} hook: it runs before
every LLM call of every agent's loop -- chat agents, sub-agents and woken
runs alike. On by default; its time limit is one second.

@{h2}What it does

It renders the text (see @{" Setting it up " link setup}) and places one
message with the configured role, marked @{tt}injected_by@{utt} with the
instance name (@{tt}simple_prompt_inject@{utt} for the shipped entry), at the
configured position. The mark is how it finds its own copy of the call
before: that copy is left alone, moved or replaced as
@{" Where the text goes " link position} describes; there is never more
than one copy of the current text.

@{h2}When it does nothing

@{bullet}The text is empty, or renders empty (an existing copy is then taken
out).
@{bullet}The call carries no message list.
@{bullet}The copy of the call before is still right: @{tt}after_system@{utt}
with the same text at the same place, @{tt}end@{utt} with the same text,
@{tt}task_start@{utt} with the same text in the task.
@{bullet}@{tt}task_start@{utt} finds no task (no user message without a
mark) or no text in it.

@{h2}Switching it off for an agent

In the agent's YAML, with the full hook name:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    overrides:
      simple_prompt_inject.inject_prompt:
        enabled: false
@{body}

The other way round, @{tt}hook_config: {enabled: false}@{utt} in the server
entry turns the hook off for all agents, and an agent's override with
@{tt}enabled: true@{utt} turns it back on for that one. Other keys in the
agent's override are ignored: the settings come only from the server entry.

@{h2}Order

The hook has no category and only asks to run between the start and the end
of the chain. In the shipped set it runs early: after
@{tt}project_instructions.withdraw_project_instructions@{utt}, before
compaction (context_engineer, context_summarizer) and before the hooks that
add messages of their own -- debate_forum's direct messages, forge's webhook
events, the project instructions. Compaction therefore sees the note like any
other message. A direct message delivered in the same call lands behind a
@{tt}before_last_user@{utt} reminder; the next call moves the reminder in
front of it.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

The rendered text, verbatim, as one message of the configured role -- no
heading, no frame. With the shipped entry that is a system message right
behind the system prompt:

@{code text}
If you start to repeat yourself with tool calls or thinking, try a different approach.
@{body}

With @{tt}task_start@{utt} it is no message of its own: the task begins
with the text, a @{tt}---@{utt} line, then the task as written.

A @{tt}developer@{utt} message goes out on the rung its client picks
(@{tt}llm/message_roles.py@{utt}): a developer turn, a @{tt}system@{utt}
turn, or a @{tt}user@{utt} turn wrapped in @{tt}<developer_note>@{utt}. A
@{tt}system@{utt} message always stands at the head because Anthropic and
Gemini have no system role inside a history and would hoist it there anyway.
There is no status line in the chat.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The text costs its size on every call; there is no size limit, so a long
file is paid for on every call. Whether it is cached depends on the place:

@{table}
Position and role | Every request a prefix of the next?
after_system, any role | yes -- until the rendered text changes
task_start (user) | yes -- until the rendered text changes
end, user | yes
end, developer | within a turn; each new turn loses the cache from the previous copy on
before_last_user, any role | within a turn; each new turn loses the cache from the previous copy on
@{body}

Measured through the hook dispatcher over two turns with a tool round each,
the session saved in between the way the agent saves it.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}One text per entry, the same for every agent that has the hook on;
a per-agent text needs its own entry.
@{bullet}No size limit on the text or the file.
@{bullet}The file is read only at start; an edit needs a restart.
@{bullet}Where the hook runs relative to compaction and the other
injections is not pinned by its schema -- see @{" The hook " link hook}.
@endnode
